.TH MAGUS-JOB 1 "" "magus" "magus Manual"
.SH NAME
magus\-job \- Fork a job, take it, return it with its result, and verify that result
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B magus
.RI "job <fork|apply|exec|exit|wait|watch|run|rm|prune|pause|resume> [flags]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Delegated work on the shell's own lifecycle. A JOB is the unit of work; a LEASE
is the grant one holder has on it: its write and read paths, plus the one check
it runs. A job is not a run: `magus run` executes a target with no job involved,
while a job's check and the server's maintenance each cause runs.
.PP
Two channels write the job store. The client MCP tool (magus\(rsjob) is an agent's, this verb
is a person's, and they reach the same store and the same rules. One author per
JOB is the property that matters, and the store enforces it: a session holding a
lease may record the base it landed on, shrink its own write paths, end its own
job in fail or no_return, and fork a child of itself inside its own paths.
Everything else, widening a boundary and verifying a job included, is refused by name.
.PP
Jobs are kept per repository rather than per checkout, so a session forking in one
worktree and a holder working in another see the same set.
.PP
fork declares one job, from flags for the one\-job case or from a JSON record on
\-\-stdin. It replaces the job it names rather than merging into it, which is the
difference between a person declaring what a job IS and an agent advancing one
field of a live one. Every path flag is repeatable or comma\-separated, and an
empty segment is refused rather than dropped. There is no \-\-state: this declares a
new job, and one nobody has taken is declared.
.PP
exec takes a job in this checkout. It records the base this tree actually landed
on beside the checkpoint the job was handed, with the divergence between them as
a fact rather than a refusal. The guard hook binds the caller when it lets exec
through, keyed on the session and subagent its agent host names, so a subagent's
exec never binds its parent; a host that names neither binds the checkout. A
caller holding a job still declared or running is refused another; once its job
has exited or ended, the next exec takes the next job.
.PP
exit returns a job with its result, FILED ONTO THE JOB so whoever waits on it
reads the same record from any checkout. The run behind the result's output ref is
resolved in the holder's own checkout and its record filed alongside, because an
output store belongs to a cache dir and nobody else can reopen it. Do the work
first, then file the result: it is a record of what happened, not a form to fill
in while you are still deciding what to do. With no \-\-stdin the job is abandoned
and recorded no_return, which is not the same as returning it and failing.
.PP
wait verifies what comes back. It reads the filed result and verifies EVIDENCE,
not claims: every changed path inside the declared write paths and outside the
denied ones, a change set that is not empty on a job that writes, descendants the
store carries, and every declared goal held: a recorded passing run for a check,
the diff since the checkpoint and the knowledge graph for paths and symbols.
Evidence must have been captured after the job was declared, so an old green run
cannot satisfy new work. A target's own run policy bounds its execution; wait does
not add a competing wall\-clock timeout. There is no field for whether the holder
thinks it passed. A job that verifies is recorded pass. Exit 1 is a status, naming
every rule that failed; exit 2 is magus unable to answer, which is a result that
would not decode, nothing filed and nothing piped in, or a job that would not
write. Whether the work is GOOD stays the reading of whoever forked it.
.PP
run submits one of the server's own jobs, the housekeeping magus does for itself,
and returns. It is a no\-op when no server is running, so a VCS hook can
call it unconditionally.
.PP
apply upserts jobs' specs from \-f <file|\->, the way kubectl apply reads a
manifest: the record is the whole spec, and the job keeps its state, holder and
registration, where a re\-fork would hand a taken job out again. Only the
orchestrator widens; a holder may apply only the release of its own paths.
.PP
rm removes one row that should never have been written. prune ENDS every job
nobody is working, as exit would abandon it, and keeps each row as the record.
.PP
pause holds jobs' holders off the machine budget: their next magus run is refused
until resume. A run already holding the budget finishes. A job's weight, in (0,1]
on its record, leans its runs' retries ahead of other waiters; it is a lean, never
a place in a queue.
.PP
Reading is elsewhere, on the verbs that read everywhere else: magus ls jobs lists
them and magus describe job prints one job's terms.
.SS job fork options
.RS
.TP
\fB\-\-brief\-format\fR \fIstring\fR
The format a brief to this job's worker takes, markdown or buzz; unset, magus.yaml's agents.brief_format decides
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR \fI<target> <project> [-- args]\fR
The one check this job runs, as <target> <project> [\-\- args] (the `magus run` is implied)
.TP
\fB\-\-checkout\fR \fIstring\fR
Make a new checkout of this repository for the job at this directory, outside the workspace; the job owns it, and magus removes it once the job ends unless it holds work
.TP
\fB\-\-checkpoint\fR \fImagus vcs checkpoint -o name\fR
The working state this job is handed, as magus vcs checkpoint \-o name prints it
.TP
\fB\-\-criteria\fR \fIstring\fR
What this job is for and what done means, as prose; the machine\-checkable half is \-\-check, and the goals a \-\-stdin record declares
.TP
\fB\-\-deny\-paths\fR \fIstring\fR
A path this job may not write, carved out of its write paths; repeatable or comma\-separated
.TP
\fB\-\-depends\-on\fR \fIstring\fR
A job this one waits on; repeatable or comma\-separated
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]fix\fR (default: off)
Make every edit a problem's fix names without judgment (add a write path, a goal or the integrator's deviation), validating again until none is left, and list only the problems that remain
.TP
\fB\-\-model\fR \fIstring\fR
The model tier, or the model, the work was matched to; unset, the workspace's matrix or default chooses the tier
.TP
\fB\-\-parent\fR \fIstring\fR
The job this one is forked from
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]read\-only\fR (default: off)
A job that gathers evidence and writes nothing
.TP
\fB\-\-read\-paths\fR \fIstring\fR
A path whose projects this job may read; repeatable or comma\-separated (additive: the written paths are readable already)
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]schema\fR (default: off)
Print the JSON schema a job must satisfy, and exit
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]stdin\fR (default: off)
Read one job as JSON on stdin instead of taking it from flags
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIduration\fR
Deny this job's writes once this long has passed since the fork (e.g. 45m, 2h); unset means no bound, unless magus.yaml sets jobs.default_timeout
.TP
\fB\-\-write\-paths\fR \fI<file>#<declaration>\fR
A path this job may write, or <file>#<declaration> to claim one declaration of a file (a path holding a literal # is spelled `./a#b.md` or `a\(rs#b.md`); repeatable or comma\-separated
.RE
.SS job apply options
.RS
.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fI-\fR
The records to apply: a file, or \- for stdin; one JSON job, a JSON array, or one job per line
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]fix\fR (default: off)
Make every edit a problem's fix names without judgment, as job fork \-\-fix does, before applying the records
.RE
.SS job exec options
.RS
.TP
\fB\-\-slots\fR \fIint\fR (default: 1)
With \-\-timeout: the machine budget slots the command takes while it runs
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIduration\fR
Run the command after \-\- and stop its whole process group once this long has passed (e.g. 20m); exits 124 at the deadline
.RE
.SS job exit options
.RS
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]schema\fR (default: off)
Print the JSON schema a result must satisfy, and exit
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]stdin\fR (default: off)
Read this job's result from stdin; without it the job is abandoned
.RE
.SS job wait options
.RS
.TP
\fB\-\-evidence\fR \fI<goal>\fR
Grade check goal <goal> against the run `<ref>` recorded in THIS checkout instead of the job's own; `check` names the job's check. The changed paths must read the same here as in the job's checkout; repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]integration\fR (default: off)
Grade only the job's check goals against the runs the \-\-stdin result names in THIS checkout, and record that beside its state
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]schema\fR (default: off)
Print the JSON schema a result must satisfy, and exit
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]stdin\fR (default: off)
Read the result from stdin instead of from the job, for one that was never filed
.RE
.SS job rm options
.RS
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]force\fR (default: off)
Remove a row that already ended, destroying the record of what happened
.RE
.SS job prune options
.RS
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]all\fR (default: off)
Also end a job a holder took and nobody touched within jobs.stale_after
.RE
.SS job pause options
.RS
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fIstring\fR
Why the job is paused, which the holder's refusal and the row show (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fIstring\fR
Lapse the pause after this long (2h) or at this RFC3339 instant; unset, it holds until resumed
.RE
.SS job grant options
.RS
.TP
\fB\-\-commit\fR \fIstring\fR
Waive the push gate for this one commit instead of lowering a rule for a job; takes only push\-ungated
.TP
\fB\-\-for\fR \fIduration\fR
End the grant after this long (e.g. 2h); unset, it lasts until the job ends
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fIstring\fR
Why the gate was skipped, recorded with the waiver; required with \-\-commit unless \-\-revoke
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]revoke\fR (default: off)
End the job's grant of the rule
.RE
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.RS
.TP
\fBfork\fR
Declare one job, from flags or a JSON record on stdin
.TP
\fBapply\fR
Upsert jobs' specs from a file or stdin, keeping each job's state
.TP
\fBexec\fR
Take the lease on a job here, and record the base this checkout landed on
.TP
\fBexit\fR
Return a job with its result, or abandon it
.TP
\fBwait\fR
Verify the result a job was exited with
.TP
\fBwatch\fR
Follow what a job's holder is doing, until interrupted
.TP
\fBrun\fR
Submit one of the server's own jobs and return
.TP
\fBrm\fR
Remove one job from the plan
.TP
\fBprune\fR
End every job nobody is working
.TP
\fBpause\fR
Hold jobs' holders off the machine budget until resumed
.TP
\fBresume\fR
Lift the pause on jobs
.TP
\fBgrant\fR
Lower one guard rule to advice for one job, typed by a person
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
\fIDeclare a job\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job fork session-load/core --write-paths internal/sessions/sessions.go --check 'test internal/sessions'
.EE
.PP
\fIDeclare it from a record\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job fork --stdin < job.json
.EE
.PP
\fITake it in this checkout\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job exec session-load/core
.EE
.PP
\fIBound a long command\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job exec --timeout 20m -- rg -l TODO ~/src
.EE
.PP
\fIReturn it with its result\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job exit session-load/core --stdin < result.json
.EE
.PP
\fIVerify what came back\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job wait session-load/core
.EE
.PP
\fIPrint the result schema\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job exit --schema
.EE
.PP
\fISubmit a server job\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job run sync-graph
.EE
.PP
\fIChange a live job's spec, keeping its state\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job apply -f job.json
.EE
.PP
\fIPreview that change\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job apply -f job.json --dry-run
.EE
.PP
\fIGrade a job's checks in a merged tree\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job wait session-load/core --integration --stdin < evidence.json
.EE
.PP
\fISee which jobs a prune would end\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job prune --dry-run
.EE
.PP
\fIHold a job's runs off the budget, then let them back\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job pause session-load/core --reason 'release build needs the machine' --until 2h && magus job resume session-load/core
.EE
.PP
\fIPush HEAD without the local gate, saying why\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job grant --commit HEAD push-ungated --reason "CI gates this release branch"
.EE
.PP
\fITake that waiver back\fR
.PP
.EX
magus job grant --commit HEAD push-ungated --revoke
.EE
.PP
